WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.290
So during my interview on yesterday's show
with Coleman Hughes, this topic of fixing

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.600
policing came up and we talked about a couple
of different ideas related to that.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.870
And one of the things that I had mentioned
was focusing in a little bit on traffic enforcement

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:19.070
as a sort of proxy to fighting a lot of what's
wrong with policing in the United States.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:22.720
And I got a bunch of e-mails about it from
people saying, how would that movie that look

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:23.720
like?

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:24.720
Can you talk about that a little more?

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.400
So let's let's do that today.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:34.620
Now, as a pretext to this, I don't have any
misconception that dealing with traffic laws

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.140
is the solution to every problem with policing
in the United States.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.520
It's not for dealing with traffic.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.010
Enforcement wouldn't do anything about lack
of training or improper training or the wrong

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.010
training.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.320
It wouldn't address qualified immunity.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.220
If that's something we want to look at, it
wouldn't, you know, deal with funding or a

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.700
whole bunch of other things.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:58.160
But there's a reason why traffic enforcement
is arguably some of the lowest hanging fruit

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.350
that we should look at right away when it
comes to policing, which is that traffic enforcement

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:09.659
is, number one, an area where police are allowed
a lot of discretion and inherent bias manifests

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:10.929
when there is discretion.

00:01:10.929 --> 00:01:17.400
And number two, traffic enforcement and traffic
related infractions are the primary way in

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.100
which Americans interact with police.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.290
By number of incidents every single year.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:28.510
And so what what I mean by this is when it
comes to traffic enforcement, we're all familiar

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:34.380
with situations in which I get pulled over
and you get pulled over and are told we were

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.939
driving 12 miles an hour over the speed limit.

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:41.570
In other words, the pretext for the stop is
exactly the same, but there might be very

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.329
different outcomes based on the discretion
of the officer.

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:50.430
So I might end up with a speeding ticket and
points on my insurance paying a higher insurance

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.149
rate for several years.

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:57.340
I might get my car searched because the officer
believes that they smell alcohol in my car

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.579
when they pull me over.

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:03.689
Meanwhile, you might get pulled over doing
the same alleged 12 miles an hour over the

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:04.810
speed limit.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:05.999
And you get a verbal warning.

00:02:05.999 --> 00:02:07.680
And I have a nice day.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:14.180
And the repercussions of the discretion that
is applied by officers to the same scenario

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:19.900
can be dramatically different, even when the
facts are essentially the same.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.739
And this could be just a result of the bias
of the police officer.

00:02:23.739 --> 00:02:28.510
Officers have a lot of discretion in terms
of who they pull over when they see somebody

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:34.769
run a stop sign, for example, once they pull
them over, who do they question more aggressively?

00:02:34.769 --> 00:02:38.610
Whose vehicles are they more interested in
searching?

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:43.879
Who do they have step out of the vehicle for
a field sobriety test when a car drives by

00:02:43.879 --> 00:02:46.129
with a tail light out.

00:02:46.129 --> 00:02:50.700
That discretion can be the difference between
an interaction that leads to a police shooting

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:56.120
or ultimately an arrest versus someone who
just drives home, not even realizing that

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.330
they drove by a police officer with their
tail light and taillight out.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:04.159
So because traffic enforcement is the most
common way that people come into contact with

00:03:04.159 --> 00:03:06.750
police every year, year in and year out.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:12.030
And it can serve as a sort of blank slate
from which investigations can start.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.000
We should be looking at this very closely.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.260
So what are some ways to reduce the discretion
which can be influenced by bias when it comes

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.159
to traffic enforcement?

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:29.250
One idea is to replace some traffic enforcement
with automation whenever possible.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:37.290
By that, we mostly mean cameras because of
course, that is a way to eliminate a bias

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.170
from an individual officer now.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.580
You can still choose to put the cameras in
certain neighborhoods and not in others.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:49.519
And you may have people who are of a certain
economic class who can better lobby to prevent

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:54.120
the cameras from coming into their neighborhoods
and push the cameras into poor neighborhoods

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.569
or black neighborhoods or whatever the case
may be.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:58.849
But it's something to look at.

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:01.349
You can have situations, you know, on highways.

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:05.209
It's typical that on highways, people everybody's
driving over the speed limit.

00:04:05.209 --> 00:04:10.920
Then you need to figure out if you're going
to replace highway speed traps with cameras.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.439
Well, how much speeding is acceptable?

00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:18.959
There's some complexities there, but at least
in some cases, automating elements of traffic

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:23.050
enforcement would dramatically reduce interactions
with police.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:27.220
And it would therefore reduce the number of
interactions that become problematic.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:34.270
Replacing traffic enforcement and speed traps
with police cameras would limit the bias of

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:39.949
an officer in leading to discretion being
used in a discriminatory way, for example.

00:04:39.949 --> 00:04:44.960
Now, it's not a replacement for fixing all
of the other things that are broken, certainly,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.960
but it would be significant nonetheless.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:53.970
So then there's the idea of having someone
other than armed police handle some traffic

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.300
enforcement.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.720
We've talked about homelessness, mental illness.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.340
Do we really need arm?

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.449
Police handling calls to nine one one related
to homelessness.

00:05:03.449 --> 00:05:06.340
It's probably better suited to some other
kind of professional.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:12.830
If we can't have better trained officers trained
like social workers, for example.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:18.060
Maybe there would be some other type of professional
that would be better suited here.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:24.740
Something analogous could be, you know, for
certain types of traffic infractions.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:28.319
You would have something other than an armed
police officer.

00:05:28.319 --> 00:05:31.370
Now, the devil is in the details, of course.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:38.169
And it's true that while the vast majority
of traffic infractions don't escalate in any

00:05:38.169 --> 00:05:43.160
way, and the ones that do sometimes escalate
because of the officer, there can be traffic

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:47.039
infractions where someone knows that they
have a warrant and it can lead to erratic

00:05:47.039 --> 00:05:48.039
behavior.

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:49.230
There's a lot of guns floating around there.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.229
So you've got you've got to think it through
right there.

00:05:51.229 --> 00:05:55.389
This is not an obvious and easy solution,
but it's something to look at.

00:05:55.389 --> 00:06:02.270
But the point is, and this is why I feel strongly
about it, when 20 million motorists per year

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:08.360
are pulled over, according to the Stanford
Policing Project, and these interactions,

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:13.860
traffic related interactions opened the door
to such a huge amount of discretion on the

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.199
part of the officer.

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:17.800
It's an area of low hanging fruit.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:24.699
We're finding some aspects to automate, finding
other areas better suited for a different

00:06:24.699 --> 00:06:28.289
type of professional than an armed police
officer.

00:06:28.289 --> 00:06:30.860
We could make a lot of progress there.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:37.139
While I don't want to ignore this, while we
look at dealing with training, qualified immunity,

00:06:37.139 --> 00:06:42.130
lack of a bad actors database at the federal
level, all of the different things that we've

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:44.610
been talking about, let me know your thoughts.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:48.360
Does this seem like an avenue that should
be pursued?

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.830
What do you see as the obstacles?

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:53.520
What do you see as the possible.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:54.770
Shortcomings of it.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:56.770
That maybe I'm ignoring.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:57.419
Let me know what you think.

